<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Sur Empire</h1><p class="st-meta">1540 CE – 1556 CE · capital Delhi</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Afghan empire of Sher Shah Suri, who drove the Mughals out of India and built roads, coinage and administration that outlived him.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sher-shah-suri">Farid Khan</a>, an Afghan noble of the Sur clan in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a>, earned the title Sher Khan and gathered power in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. He defeated the Mughal emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humayun">Humayun</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-chausa">Chausa</a> in 1539 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannauj">Kannauj</a> in 1540, drove him into exile and proclaimed himself Sher Shah, ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> over north India.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sher-shah-suri">Sher Shah</a> reorganised revenue on measured land, introduced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rupiya">rupiya</a> that gave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rupee">rupee</a> its name, built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-trunk-road">Grand Trunk Road</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravanserai">caravanserais</a> and a postal service, and built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rohtas-fort">Rohtas Fort</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/purana-qila">Purana Qila</a>. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> Shah continued centralised rule, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hemu">Hemu</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> general, served his successors.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sher-shah-suri">Sher Shah</a> died in an explosion at the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalinjar-fort">Kalinjar</a> in 1545. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> Shah's death in 1554 the empire split among rival claimants. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humayun">Humayun</a> returned in 1555, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar's</a> victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-battle-of-panipat">Second Battle of Panipat</a> in 1556 restored the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/sur-empire.jpg" alt="Sur Empire" width="320" height="296"><figcaption>Image: Map created from DEMIS Mapserver, which are public domain. Koba-chan Borders by Noorullah21 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Sur_Empire_at_its_height.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1540 CE – 1556 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Delhi</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Sher Shah Suri, Islam Shah Suri, Adil Shah Suri</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Persian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>India, Pakistan, Bangladesh</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Suri dynasty, Sur dynasty, Suri Empire</dd></dl></aside></div>

